In this section, we introduce the proof that if $f\in C_0^oo(\R^d)$ and $u =
(\lambda - \Delta)^{-1}f$, then $u$ and all derivatives tends to zero
exponentially as $|x| -> oo$. More precisely, for every multiindex $\alpha$,
$$
 |D^\alpha u(x)| <= N e^{-\mu|x|},
$$
where $N = N(\alpha,d,\lambda,f) > 0$ and $\mu = \mu(\lambda) > 0$.

To prove this, observe that
$$
    G_\lambda(x) \defeq \int_0^oo e^{-\lambda t}p(t,x)dt,\quad
    p(t,x) \defeq {1//(4\pi t)^{d/2}}\exp ?(-{|x|^2//4t})?.
$$
is the Green's function to $(\lambda-\Delta)^{-1}$.

Let $\mu>0$ be a constant specified in later. By Young's inequality,
$$
    \mu|x| <= {\epsilon // 8t}|x|^2 + 2t\mu^2,\quad
    e^{\mu|x|}p(t,x)
    <= (4\pi t)^{-d/2}\exp
    ?(-{|x|^2//8t})? e^{2t\mu^2}.
$$
By integrating over $x$, we have\footnote{
    It is easy to calculate $\int_{\R^d}e^{-a|x|^2}dx = (\pi/a)^{d/2}$ where
    $a>0$.
}
$$
    ||e^{\mu|\cdot|}p(t,\cdot)||_1 <= 2^{d/2} e^{2t\mu^2}.
$$
If we take $\mu$ such that $2\mu^2 - \lambda < 0$, we therefore obtain
$$
    ||e^{\mu|\cdot|}G_\lambda||_1 <= \int_0^oo e^{-\lambda
    t}||e^{\mu|\cdot|}p(t,\cdot)||_1 dt <= N(d) (\lambda - 2\mu^2).
$$
Since $G_\lambda$ is the Green's function, if $u = (\lambda-\Delta)^{-1}f$, we
have $u = G_\lambda * f$. Therefore,
$$
    e^{\mu|x|}|u(x)|
    <= \int_{\R^d}e^{\mu|x|}|G_\lambda(x-y)| |f(y)| dy
    <= \int_{\R^d} (e^{\mu|x-y|}|G_\lambda(x-y)|) (e^{\mu|y|}|f(y)|) dy
    <= ||e^{\mu|\cdot|}f||_oo ||e^{\mu|\cdot|}G_\lambda||_1,
$$
$$
    |u(x)| <= N(d,\lambda,f) e^{-\mu|x|}.
$$

Similarly, since $D^\alpha u = G_\lambda * D^\alpha f$, we can obtain the
similar result. Here, the constant $N$ also depends on $\alpha$.
